.tl 'UNSW Distribution tape''\\*t'
.sp 2
.ns
..
.de FO
.bp
..
.pl 12i
.ds t Format
.wh 0 HD
.wh -4 FO
.sp 2
.cu
.ce
FORMAT OF THIS DISTRIBUTION TAPE
.sp 2
The distribution tape was created with 'dtp'
- essentially a modified 'tp'.
The reason for the departure from standard Unix 'tp' is
that this tape contains all of our collected source, with many more
files than 'tp' can write, and with many more blocks than can be
comfortably written with 512 byte records.
.sp 1
'dtp' solves these problems well.
It writes blocks of 16K bytes and allows
for (almost) unlimited numbers of files.
These files can be extracted on
a directory or individual basis.
Dtp also has a create directory mode,
for extracting arbitrary chunks of
a file system
(no more mkdirs).
'dtp' is used just like 'tp' except for some perverse reason
some of the flags used to control 'dtp' are different.
'dtp.5' and 'dtp.8' are attached
the diferences with 'tp' should be obvious.
'dtp' will run on the standard version 6 Unix system.
.sp 1
A listing produced by dtp of the distribution tape has been included
in the package. You can scan it to isolate just what you are interested
in from the distribution.
.sp 1
The format of this distribution tape is as follows.
.sp 1
.nf
.in +7
 start     end     size          description
record   record

    1 -->         16K bytes      'dtp' directory.
      -->         16K bytes      'dtp' dumped files.

  TAPE MARK

    1 -->  14     512 bytes      copy of 'dtp' a.out.

  TAPE MARK

    1 --> 54      512 bytes      copy of 'dtp' source.

  TAPE MARK
  TAPE MARK
.in -7
.fi
.sp 1
The ~~~~~~~~ record also contains a copy
of the dtp program ('dtp' is the first
thing in the dump, so it is the first thing after the directory).
The 'dtp' in the second tape file is identical to this one.
The source of dtp is in the third tape file in case your system has changed
in some dramatic way from standard Unix, such that this version of dtp will
not run. The source for our version of dtp can be found somewhere in the dump.
Dtp will extract it. See the copy
of dtp(V) and dtp(VIII) enclosed.
.ds t Contents
.bp
.ce
.cu
CONTENTS OF TAPE
.sp 1
The 'dtp' part of the tape was created with the command 'dtp nm0 dtp usr'.
Thus it can be extracted partially or in full into any directory
on your system called 'usr'.
.sp 1
The first task to be accomplished is to extract
from the tape the 'dtp' program so that you can then extract
the various files and directories from the tape.
As you can see two copies of 'dtp' are stored on the tape
either will do.
The following two commands can be used to extract firstly
the one from the first file or secondly the copy from the second file.
.sp 1
.nf
	dd if=/dev/rmt0 bs=16k of=dtp skip=     count=1
	chmod 700 dtp
.sp 1
	dd if=/dev/rmt0 bs=1b of=dtp conv=noerror skip=      count=14
	chmod 700 dtp
.sp 1
.fi
.sp 1
If you posess a filesystem with 19,000 free blocks
and can create a 600 block file in /tmp
for the 'dtp' workarea
then to extract all the files on this tape,
mount tape on unit rmt0
and use the following commands:
.nf
.in +7
.sp 1
mkdir usr
dtp xcm0		*** Xtract - Create directories - 800bpi - unit=0
.in -7
.fi
.sp 1
If you posess a filesystem with 20,000 free blocks
and can't create a 600 block file in /tmp
then to extract all the files on this tape,
mount tape on unit rmt0
and use the following commands:
.nf
.in +7
.sp 1
mkdir usr
dtp xcm0t temp		*** Xtract - Create directories - 800bpi - unit=0
			*** temp is used by 'dtp' as work area.
.sp 1
.in -7
.fi
If only a specific directory and its sub-directories
is required then the following precedures can be followed.
With the tape comes a listing of the contents of the tape
this listing was created by:
.br
	dtp lm0 | pr -2 -h "UNSW - third distribution" > /dev/lp
.br
To get a full listing of the contents you could use the following
command:
.br
	dtp lmv0
.br
BOTH of the above assume that a 600 block file can be created
in /tmp.
If you can't create a 600 block file in /tmp then
to get a full listing use:
.br
	dtp lmv0t temp
.br
This requires that you can create a 600 block file (temp)
in the current directory.
.sp 1
In what follows no
mention will be made of the 'dtp' temp file
but you can always specify it.
.sp 1
As you can see from the tape contents the directory usr
has seven subdirectories:
.nf
	docmention	full and complete documentation directories
	lib		all the libraries currently in use at UNSW
	binaries		some necessary binaries - esp if new C-compiler to be used
	include		contains miscellaneous .h files
	misc.doc		contains all the documentation included with tape
	source		entire source directories
	sys		our 'UNIX'
.fi
.sp 1
If you don't have enough space for a full extraction
of the tape contents or you merely wish to view sections
of it a little at a time then what follows will help.
.sp 1
If you only wish to extract the sys directory
then use the following commands:
.br
	mkdir usr
.br
	dtp xmc0 usr/sys
.sp 1
If you only wish to extract the source/s1 directory
then use the following commands:
.br
	mkdir usr
.br
	dtp xmc0 usr/source/s1
.sp 1
If you only wish to extract a single file, say usr/sys/dmr/lp.c
then use the following commands:
.br
	mkdir usr usr/sys usr/sys/dmr
.br
	dtp xm0 /usr/sys/dmr/lp.c
.sp 1
Tape i/o errors hopefully will not happen but if they do
then the 'i' option of 'dtp' can be used to ignore them,
but results in this case cannot be guarenteed.
